{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1794644619,"nonce":"ELPUfzl4xn3OC7Gu9TV51IjaBms=","target":"82cb190b6510b8bb5c730b434b24e30297c90a75b1142b8002c70916b70ee019","message":"A fade on rows needs no group only where the node paints one primitive (`RenderTree::fades_without_group`, ff66023). A node with a border, shadow, children or text under it paints draws that overlap, and fading each draw by itself is a different picture from fading them as one: a label over a fill shows the fill through its edges. So it keeps a group when its opacity is bound (an offscreen layer, about 0.3 ms each in a frame), and a CSS animation of its opacity is baked into a texture per node.\n\nMost of cn's opacity keyframes are on nodes like this: tooltip, popover, dropdown, context menu, hover card, navigation menu and dialog enters, the tab slides, fade in and out. Each is one node at a time, so the cost is a texture and a group, not a page of them; it matters when several are animating, or on a phone where the texture is what the memory goes to.\n\nOptions to weigh:\n- A layer the node is painted into once and the row fades, so the subtree is not painted again and the layer is one texture of the node's size. This is the same texture the baked path holds, so the saving is the CPU of re-baking, not the memory.\n- A node with a fill and a border is one primitive. `fades_without_group` excludes the border because how the shader composites the border over the fill under a per-colour alpha has not been checked against a group; if the two match, a bordered node qualifies.\n- Text over a fill: the glyph rows take an alpha ratio, but the result is the per-draw picture above, not the group's.\n\nRelated: ff66023 (closed), 54771c4 (P5, paint tracks), 0623714 (CSS transitions).","files":null}]}